WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.833
uv is one program that replaces
a whole shelf of Python tools.

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:08.046
It installs packages,
it builds virtual environments,

00:00:08.046 --> 00:00:11.938
it resolves a lockfile,
and it installs Python itself.

00:00:12.261 --> 00:00:15.976
It is written in Rust, and it keeps
one cache for your whole machine,

00:00:15.976 --> 00:00:18.634
so a wheel you have already
downloaded is linked

00:00:18.634 --> 00:00:21.760
into a new project rather
than fetched twice.

00:00:22.048 --> 00:00:25.591
That is why a cold install
finishes in seconds.

00:00:26.566 --> 00:00:29.492
Before any of that,
uv has to be on your machine.

00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:31.837
Install it with the standalone installer,

00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:34.487
which needs no Python
on the machine first.

00:00:34.751 --> 00:00:39.711
On macOS and Linux, pipe the script
from astral dot sh into your shell.

00:00:39.999 --> 00:00:42.892
On Windows, run the same
script through powershell.

00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:47.397
Homebrew and pip ex both carry uv as well,
and either one is fine.

00:00:47.626 --> 00:00:51.751
From then on, uv self update
moves you to the newest release.

00:00:53.009 --> 00:00:55.995
With uv installed,
the next step is a project.

00:00:56.074 --> 00:00:59.269
Run uv init and give your project a name.

00:00:59.778 --> 00:01:04.004
uv writes four files:
a pyproject dot toml that

00:01:04.004 --> 00:01:07.753
declares the project,
a read me, a starter module,

00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:13.073
and a dot python dash version file that
pins the interpreter this project wants.

00:01:13.454 --> 00:01:18.112
Nothing is installed yet, and there
is no virtual environment yet either.

00:01:19.038 --> 00:01:23.441
uv builds one for you the first
time you ask it to run something.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.813
An empty project needs packages,
so add your first one.

00:01:28.077 --> 00:01:32.271
uv add httpx is how a package
joins your project.

00:01:32.814 --> 00:01:37.066
It resolves the dependency,
writes it into pyproject dot toml,

00:01:37.075 --> 00:01:41.571
updates the lockfile, and installs
it into the environment, all in one step.

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:44.865
For a package you need only
while developing, name the group,

00:01:44.865 --> 00:01:48.650
so uv add dash dash
group dev pytest records

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.149
pytest under the dependency groups table.

00:01:51.727 --> 00:01:53.723
uv remove takes a package back out,

00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:56.408
and the lockfile and the
environment both follow it.

00:01:57.733 --> 00:02:00.568
Every one of those steps
touched the lockfile.

00:02:00.624 --> 00:02:04.084
uv lock writes a lockfile
that pins every package,

00:02:04.084 --> 00:02:07.430
for every platform your project
claims to support.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.804
uv sync then makes your environment
match that lockfile exactly,

00:02:11.804 --> 00:02:15.185
installing what is missing and removing
whatever does not belong.

00:02:15.264 --> 00:02:17.772
Commit the lockfile, because it is what

00:02:17.772 --> 00:02:20.944
makes the install
reproducible for everybody else.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.320
In continuous integration,
pass the frozen flag,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.073
so uv installs straight from
the lockfile and never resolves it again.

00:02:29.608 --> 00:02:32.873
With everything in sync,
it is time to run something.

00:02:32.975 --> 00:02:36.330
uv run runs a command inside
your project's environment,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:41.116
and it syncs before it starts, so what
runs is always what the lockfile says.

00:02:41.276 --> 00:02:45.086
uvx runs a tool once without
installing it anywhere at all.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.825
uv tool install keeps a tool
on your path for good.

00:02:48.857 --> 00:02:51.063
And a single file script
can declare its own

00:02:51.063 --> 00:02:53.466
dependencies in a comment
header at the top,

00:02:53.466 --> 00:02:55.718
so running it builds
an environment just for that

00:02:55.718 --> 00:02:57.857
file and throws it away afterwards.

00:02:59.263 --> 00:03:02.958
So that is uv,
and your project fits on one card.

00:03:03.338 --> 00:03:07.169
uv init starts it,
uv add grows it, uv lock pins it,

00:03:07.169 --> 00:03:10.481
uv sync matches it, and uv run uses it.

00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:14.184
Five commands, one tool.
Go clear the shelf.
